From 277331d4945b626d01ebb5951cd333c431975883 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Vanik <ben.vanik@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 07:53:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [Loom] Make spill materialization converge by construction
 (#1398)

Spill materialization now converges through an explicit progress
invariant instead of stopping after eight batches. The fixed ceiling
could reject programs that were still retiring distinct spill
candidates; the gfx1201 kernel from #1391 reaches a valid spill-free
frame after 14 batches.

## Design

Before each batch rewrites IR, the emission frame adds every current
spill-plan value to its persistent required-register set. AMDGPU
terminal lowering extends that set with every helper it creates and
every existing source exposed by the rewrite, while rematerialized
clones inherit the requirement from their source.

A completed batch therefore retires distinct values from the spillable
population. Later allocations cannot recursively spill the traffic that
implements an earlier spill. This produces two meaningful outcomes:

- Allocation reaches zero spill plans and emits the kernel.
- The accumulated required-register set cannot fit, and allocation
reports `BACKEND/005` with the register class, budget, peak, and
`spill-traffic-register-exhausted` failure code.

The old `BACKEND/021` final-frame diagnostic represented the numerical
ceiling and two impossible producer states, so this removes it with the
dead retry machinery.

## Behavior

| Case | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Progress continues beyond batch 8 | Rejected with
`spill-materialization-iteration-limit` | Continues until allocation has
no spill plans |
| Required spill traffic exceeds the register budget | Could be masked
by the iteration limit | Reports `spill-traffic-register-exhausted` at
the allocation boundary |

## Evidence

- The reported gfx1201 kernel now compiles after 14 batches,
materializing 52 spill slots occupying 936 bytes into a 21 KiB HSACO.
- A focused authored Low regression needs ten batches; restoring the old
ceiling reproduces the batch-eight failure. A separate over-budget case
verifies terminal register exhaustion.
- Optimized `loom-compile` shrinks by 1,336 file bytes and 1,168
text-class bytes; initialized data shrinks by 32 bytes and actual `.bss`
is unchanged.
- The full Loom presubmit and focused ASAN coverage pass.

Fixes #1391.

Backport of upstream d8e8ae41ec513b869465f6229965665e4eb4dae7 (#1398)
onto 5927b0e0. It needs upstream 4b1c67c7e7 (#277), backported in the
previous commit, for the required-register bitmap that allocation honors
and AMDGPU spill lowering extends. This revision bounds every frame
repair with one shared eight-round counter; spill-materialization
batches no longer advance it, so it bounds only rematerialization,
live-range splitting and pair-replication trials, and the batch count
remains in the repair statistics. The materialization result's storage
count equals the plan count here as well.
allocation_json.loom-test emits spill_materialization_converges as
native code instead of a with-checks allocation-json case, which this
loom-check cannot run, with 104 VGPRs: this revision's allocator reports
the honest spill-traffic register exhaustion below that, and before this
change it stops at the eight-round limit for 104 through 108.
low_allocation.loom-test's live_in_split_follows_entry_preamble case no
longer pins %group: with spilled values kept in registers, pinning both
%kernarg and %group leaves no room for spill traffic in 7 SGPRs. %kernarg
still splits after the whole live-in preamble and %group's spill traffic
leaves the workitem id in v0.
